==English==

===Alternative forms===
*[[noodnick]], [[noodnik]], [[nudnick]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|yi|נודניק}} < root of {{m|yi|נודיען||to bore}} + {{m|yi|־ניק||noun-forming suffix}} (English {{m|en|-nik}}). Ultimately from {{der|en|sla-pro|*nuda}} < {{der|en|ine-pro|*newti-||need}} < {{m|ine-pro|*new-||death, to be exhausted}}.

Compare {{cog|ru|ну́дный||tedious}}, {{cog|uk|нудни́й||tedious}}, {{cog|pl|nudny||boring}}, {{cog|sk|nudný||boring}}, {{cog|cu|ноудити}} or {{m|cu|нѫдити||to compel}}, {{cog|he|נוּדְנִיק||[[nag#Verb|nag]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈnʊdnɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʊdnɪk|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|colloquial|sometimes|attributive}} A person who is very [[annoy]]ing; a [[pest]], a [[nag]], a [[jerk]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#*'''1992''', Richard Preston quoting Samuel Eilenberg, ''[[:w:The New Yorker|The New Yorker]]'', 2 March, [https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1992/03/02/the-mountains-of-pi "The Mountains of Pi"]:
#*:He interrupts people, and he is not interested in anything except what concerns him and his brother. He is a '''nudnick'''!
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1962|author={{w|Philip K. Dick}}|chapter=The Man in the High Castle|title=Four Novels of the 1960s|publisher=Library of America|year_published=2007|page=15
|passage=Juliana greeted strangers with a portentous, '''nudnik''', Mona Lisa smile that hung them up between responses, whether to say hello or not.}}

